0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,770


1
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:07,060
There are many physical problems in which a body undergoes

2
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:09,770
motion about a central point.

3
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:12,350
And when that happens, there's a natural coordinate system

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:15,150
to describe that motion, which in two dimensions

5
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:16,820
is polar coordinates.

6
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:19,390
So let's consider the orbit of an object.

7
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:22,172
For instance, the best example is a circular orbit.

8
00:00:22,172 --> 00:00:24,770
If we have a circular orbit of an object,

9
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:27,632
there is a central point, which we'll call P.

10
00:00:27,632 --> 00:00:31,920
Now given this type of motion with an object,

11
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,770
it naturally makes sense to choose a coordinate system

12
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:36,540
called polar coordinates.

13
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:39,200
The way that coordinate system works is as follows.

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,440
First off, we need to choose a reference angle.

15
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:45,510
And so we'll choose a horizontal line.

16
00:00:45,510 --> 00:00:47,310
And we'll draw a ray.

17
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:50,880
And we'll show a direction of increasing reference angle

18
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:52,140
theta.

19
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:56,050
In this example, theta will go from 0 to 2 pi.

20
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:58,650


21
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:01,980
Along with the reference angle, we have a distance

22
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:03,390
from the central point.

23
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:06,910
And that distance we'll refer to as r.

24
00:01:06,910 --> 00:01:10,910
So the coordinates of our point are r and theta.

25
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:14,960
Now the variable r is always greater than zero

26
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,520
and can go to infinity.

27
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,990
So this is our polar coordinate system.

28
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:23,930
When you have a coordinate system,

29
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:26,420
remember, at every point in space,

30
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,440
there has to be unit vectors.

31
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,830
So at this point right here, how do we choose unit vectors

32
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:35,170
for polar coordinates?

33
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:37,240
We always choose the unit vectors

34
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:41,620
to point in the increasing direction of the coordinate.

35
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,030
Take the r-coordinate.

36
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:45,040
That increases radially outward.

37
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:49,390
So our unit vectors here will have a r hat

38
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:52,970
pointing radially outward.

39
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:55,660
What about the theta direction?

40
00:01:55,660 --> 00:02:00,940
Tangential to the circle, in this particular case.

41
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:05,390
Because theta is increasing in this direction,

42
00:02:05,390 --> 00:02:09,190
we choose our tangential unit vector,

43
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:12,600
which we're going to call theta hat, which is at right angles

44
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:16,460
to r hat, to point in the direction of increasing theta.

45
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:21,130
And so at this point, we now have a set of unit vectors.

46
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:24,170
Now one has to be very careful in polar coordinates

47
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:25,720
for the following reason.

48
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:29,420
Suppose that you're at another point over here.

49
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:31,740
Now because we have two different points,

50
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:33,660
let's just give some names to these points.

51
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:37,300
We'll call this s1 and this point s2.

52
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:41,110
And the unit vector's over here where r1 and theta were.

53
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:43,300
When we're at the point as 2, we have

54
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:46,240
to choose unit vectors exactly the same way.

55
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:52,455
r hat 2 points in the direction of increasing r, and theta hat

56
00:02:52,455 --> 00:02:56,290
2 points in the direction of increasing theta.

57
00:02:56,290 --> 00:02:58,210
So what we see in polar coordinates

58
00:02:58,210 --> 00:03:02,150
is that r hat 1 is not equal to r hat 2.

59
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:03,420
Why are they not equal?

60
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:05,720
They both are unit vectors, so they both have

61
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:09,950
the same magnitude, but they point in opposite directions,

62
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:14,660
in the same way that theta hat 1 is not equal to theta hat 2.

63
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,950
So unlike Cartesian coordinates, in which at every single point

64
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:21,210
had the same unit vectors, in polar coordinates,

65
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:24,830
the unit vectors depend on where you are in space.

66
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:27,830
And that will make our analysis on polar coordinates

67
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:30,039
a little bit more complicated.

68
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:30,539


